A||||9||Neal||Replacement for Displacment?||||1||
Z||000000||Neal||06-04-2001||03:01 PM||||For a long time now there has been the statement "no replacement for displacement" but in the last few years huge hp have been comign from small production engines. eg S2000, new Celica, and many others.  But what these cars dont have is the low end grunt that the larger "american" engines have.  Thus the argument remains.<P>There are a few possible replacements for displacement.<BR>Turbos<BR>Superchargers<BR>And High Revs<P>Basicly It all comes down to getting the most air possible into a given displacement over a givven time.  an engine will flow 2X as much air at 15 psi of boost and the same can be said about airflow at 8000RPM and 4000RPM<P>While technology is good and allows for more engine flexability, i am still a believer in displacement, to a degree (i would rather rev my engine to 6000 and get less tq than have a 4000 redline and get gigantic tq numbers).  But the s2000 as an example, is from many testimonials, a total dog untill wound right up, and really who wants to drive a street car over 5000rpm all day.  AND when the s2000 has its 2L engine at 5-9 thousand rpm wouldnt the gas milage be FAR worse than say a buic 3800, which makes a little less hp but more tq at much lower RPM?<P>Turbos and supers are a good ways to combat being forced to drive in the upper RPM range for power, but they do have a good habit of killing fuel economy, and requiring better gas and higher maintence.<P>Myself i like revs and i like turbos.  Im less a fan of superchargers (less efficient than a turbo).  But i also like displacement.<P>No flames intended, just being open to dissusion.||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||06-04-2001||03:08 PM||||I like the turbo route myself. Gave me a real horsepower boost without doing any expensive internal work. Mines set to start coming on boost about 2500 rpm, so my midrange is where I notice the most performance increase. I use reg 87 gas for 80K miles and get around 200 miles on a fillup. Had no mechanical failures. Just the usual coil, ign probs. Now if I could just get the trans to shift by itself.......<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||06-04-2001||03:44 PM||||MY whole attitude is the following.<BR>Part for part, there is no replacement for displacement.<BR>YES a turbo'ed small displacement motor can outperform a NA V8, but turbo'ing the V8 will result in the small displacement motor being below the V8 for power.  Sure there is balancing of the motor etc...  but "mod for mod" it seems that the general rule is that more displacement will win.<BR>There are always other items such as overhead cam vs pushrod, or aluminum vs iron.<BR>Then there is the weight factor, and that can change which is better for the overall performance of the car. <P>Personally, "Whatever floats your boat" is a good guideline for motor selection.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||06-04-2001||05:20 PM||||There are so many ways to get power out of an engine that the combinations are almost limitless.  It comes down to everybodies personal tastes and driving style.  I like engines with a lot of torque since I do a lot of stop and go city driving.  Its more enjoyable with some low end grunt.  A Celica that has to be wound to 6000 RPM's before the aggressive Cam takes over will seem like a real dog in this type of driving.  On the other hand, it certainly can be entertaining with a long stretch of highway to fly down! <P>If we get into the mods question, then we have to admit that they can be done to any engine.  Just look what some of these big time tuners like Rousch or Hennessey do to Corvettes, Vipers, and Mustangs.  They may not be able to scream at 8000 RPM, but they are still fast and I am sure enjoyable tio drive fast!||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||06-04-2001||07:20 PM||||Turbo's and superchargers are great until you drive something with some cubes.<P>In the end, there is no replacement for displacement.||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||06-04-2001||09:49 PM||||Turbos are the way to go.My 3700 lb. car gets over 20 mpg on the highway with a 389 ford 9" rear and a 231 cubic inch v6.With the stock 343 rear it got about 23 mpg on the highway and ran 115mph in the quarter mile.That speed computes to about 441 hp,which is really pushing the limit of what the head gaskets will support. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||06-04-2001||10:17 PM||||If we're talking torque (and we should be), positive displacement superchargers are way more productive than a turbo. You get boost at idle, the boost does not get disproportionately larger with engine speed (as with centrifugal), and they produce torque at low revs. <BR>This is my idea of the only pressurization to consider. IMHO.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||06-04-2001||10:47 PM||||Triad,<BR> 1st I don't believe blowers are capable of generating boost at idle.<BR> 2nd Turbo boost is controlled by a wastegate that limits total boost.<BR> 3rd torque is a result of many factors,boost being only one of them.<BR> To raise the horsepower of an engine with a blower,it is necessary to generate enough horsepower to drive the blower and then the excess is left as an increase in net horsepower.<BR> The horsepower generated by a turbo is basically free,using the exhaust to drive the compressor.<P> The way I see it,the blower must help the engine make as much as 150 hp to realize a net increase of 100.The horsepower made by a turbo has no parasitic loses.It is less stressful on an engine to make 100hp than 150hp and the net result is the same.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierogt88||06-05-2001||10:18 AM||||Hmmm, interesting topic for me.  As some of you may remember from my far-too-infrequent posts, I have been working on a plan for a 4.3 supercharged to put into my 88 GT.  So, for the experts out there, I really would like the skinny on turbo vs supercharged.  I believe that displacement is the #1 factor, but once you run out of room for displacement then you start pumping the air...<P>So, I have been lead to believe through my experiences that supercharging will put the power out through a much wider band, and save money on modifying the exhast, but that turbo is more efficient and powerful once you get to the higher rpm's.  Since the 4.3 is a lower-rev powerplant, and my gt is an automatic (meaning less control of my rpm's) I would think supercharging it is the way to go.  Thoughts, opinions, criticizms, and flames are welcome, since this project is still in the planning phase.<P>BTW, for those considering a 4.3 swap I have discovered that the 86 to 88 Monte Carlos came with 4.3's, and since it was in the LS models rather than the SS models, should be fairly easy to find a donor car.||||reg||5
Z||000009||Neal||06-05-2001||02:39 PM||||As far as turbos go where and when you get the power is very much a result of engeneering in the project.  Look at the VW/Audi 1.8 turbo it gets boost with little to no lag at ANY driving rpm and has one of the flattest TQ curves of any engine.<BR>So basicly if the turbo system is carfully designed it wont give you high rpm power only||||reg||1
